---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base title="Resilience" description="Why Bolivia during global uncertainty -- an honest, research-backed analysis of strengths, weaknesses, and what the Yungas specifically offers.">

  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="relative h-[50vh] min-h-[420px] flex items-end overflow-hidden">
    <div class="absolute inset-0 bg-gradient-to-br from-bark-800 via-bark-900 to-bark-900"></div>
    <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 80%, var(--color-accent) 0%, transparent 50%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 20%, var(--color-cyan) 0%, transparent 50%);"></div>
    <div class="absolute inset-0 bg-gradient-to-t from-bark-900/95 via-bark-900/40 to-transparent"></div>
    <div class="relative z-10 max-w-[1400px] mx-auto px-6 lg:px-10 pb-14 w-full">
      <span class="text-label text-accent">Resilience</span>
      <h1 class="text-headline text-bark-50 mt-2 text-split">Why Bolivia in a Crisis</h1>
      <p class="text-body-lg text-bark-300 mt-3 max-w-2xl">
        Not the safest place on Earth. Not a bunker fantasy.
        A place with real advantages, real vulnerabilities, and a community learning to live well in precarious times.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Executive Summary -->
  <section class="py-24 px-6 lg:px-10">
    <div class="max-w-[1400px] mx-auto">
      <div class="max-w-3xl">
        <h2 class="text-title text-bark-800 mb-6 reveal">The Honest Verdict</h2>
        <p class="text-bark-600 text-[1.1rem] leading-[1.85] mb-6 reveal" data-delay="100">
          Bolivia has genuine safety advantages during global crises &mdash; geographic remoteness
          from conflict zones, no nuclear targets, low baseline violence, and indigenous community
          resilience structures that predate the state by centuries. These are not marketing claims.
          They are verifiable facts.
        </p>
        <p class="text-bark-600 text-[1.1rem] leading-[1.85] mb-6 reveal" data-delay="200">
          But the claim that Bolivia is "one of the safest places on Earth" requires heavy qualification.
          Its current economic collapse (reserves near $100M, 20%+ inflation, currency crisis),
          depleting energy sector, food import dependency, weak healthcare, and fragile infrastructure
          create vulnerabilities that could <em>amplify</em> &mdash; not buffer &mdash; the effects of
          a global crisis.
        </p>

        <blockquote class="border-l-3 border-accent pl-6 py-4 bg-accent/5 rounded-r-xl reveal" data-delay="300">
          <p class="text-bark-700 font-display font-medium text-[1.15rem] leading-relaxed italic">
            Bolivia is a place where you are unlikely to be bombed, but where you might struggle
            to buy medicine, fuel, or flour. The Yungas specifically is better than urban Bolivia &mdash;
            but it is not New Zealand.
          </p>
        </blockquote>

        <p class="text-bark-600 text-[1.1rem] leading-[1.85] mt-6 reveal" data-delay="400">
          This page lays out the full analysis &mdash; strengths, weaknesses, scenario ratings &mdash;
          so you can make your own judgment. All figures are sourced from UNODC, World Bank, IMF,
          and government reports. Where data is uncertain, we say so.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="divider-accent"></div>

  <!-- Safety Factors -->
  <section class="py-24 px-6 lg:px-10">
    <div class="max-w-[1400px] mx-auto">
      <h2 class="text-title text-bark-800 mb-3 reveal">What Bolivia Gets Right</h2>
      <p class="text-bark-500 mb-12 max-w-2xl reveal" data-delay="100">
        Safety factors ranked from strongest to weakest. Evidence first, interpretation second.
      </p>

      <!-- Factor 1: Nuclear -->
      <div class="mb-12 reveal" data-delay="150">
        <div class="flex items-start gap-4 mb-4">
          <div class="flex-shrink-0 w-12 h-12 rounded-xl bg-accent/10 flex items-center justify-center">
            <span class="font-display font-bold text-accent text-lg">1</span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 flex-wrap">
              <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800 text-lg">No Nuclear or Strategic Military Value</h3>
              <span class="tag tag-accent">Strong</span>
            </div>
            <p class="text-bark-600 text-[1rem] leading-[1.8] mt-3">
              Zero nuclear weapons. Zero foreign military bases. Constitution explicitly prohibits them.
              Signatory to every major nuclear disarmament treaty (NPT 1970, TPNW 2019).
              Member of the Non-Aligned Movement &mdash; no NATO, no mutual defense pacts.
              In nuclear war targeting models, Bolivia does not appear on any published target list.
            </p>
            <p class="text-bark-600 text-[1rem] leading-[1.8] mt-3">
              Studies modeling nuclear winter food production identify South American countries as among
              the most likely to sustain civilization post-exchange. This is Bolivia's strongest card.
            </p>
            <p class="text-bark-400 text-xs mt-3">
              Sources:
              <a href="https://opanal.org/en/bolivia/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-accent/70 hover:text-accent underline">OPANAL</a>,
              <a href="https://www.icanw.org/bolivia" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-accent/70 hover:text-accent underline">ICAN</a>,
              <a href="https://www.armscontrol.org/act/2019-09/news-briefs/bolivia-ratifies-ban-treaty" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-accent/70 hover:text-accent underline">Arms Control Association</a>
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Factor 2: Geographic Remoteness -->
      <div class="mb-12 reveal" data-delay="150">
        <div class="flex items-start gap-4 mb-4">
          <div class="flex-shrink-0 w-12 h-12 rounded-xl bg-accent/10 flex items-center justify-center">
            <span class="font-display font-bold text-accent text-lg">2</span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 flex-wrap">
              <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800 text-lg">Geographic Remoteness from Conflict Zones</h3>
              <span class="tag tag-accent">Strong</span>
            </div>
            <p class="text-bark-600 text-[1rem] leading-[1.8] mt-3">
              Center of South America. Bordered by five countries, none engaged in or likely to enter
              a major international conflict. Thousands of kilometers from any active war zone, any
              great-power projection theater, any significant naval chokepoint.
            </p>
            <p class="text-bark-600 text-[1rem] leading-[1.8] mt-3">
              South America as a whole has been interstate-war-free since the Cenepa War
              (Ecuador-Peru, 1995) &mdash; making the entire continent one of the most peaceful
              regions on Earth for conventional warfare. Bolivia is insulated by multiple layers
              of neutral or friendly nations.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Factor 3: Low Violence -->
      <div class="mb-12 reveal" data-delay="150">
        <div class="flex items-start gap-4 mb-4">
          <div class="flex-shrink-0 w-12 h-12 rounded-xl bg-accent/10 flex items-center justify-center">
            <span class="font-display font-bold text-accent text-lg">3</span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 flex-wrap">
              <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800 text-lg">Low Baseline Violence</h3>
              <span class="tag" style="border-color: var(--color-accent); color: var(--color-accent-dim);">Moderate-Strong</span>
            </div>
            <p class="text-bark-600 text-[1rem] leading-[1.8] mt-3">
              Bolivia's homicide rate of <strong class="text-bark-800">4.1–4.3 per 100,000</strong> (2023, estimates vary by source) is among the
              lowest in Latin America &mdash; comparable to Chile and lower than the United States (6.4).
              During the 2019 political crisis, the overall annual rate actually <em>declined</em>.
              During COVID-19 (2020), it dropped to 3.49, the lowest recorded.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Homicide comparison -->
        <div class="overflow-x-auto ml-16 mt-4">
          <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse rounded-xl overflow-hidden border border-bark-200">
            <thead class="bg-bark-800">
              <tr>
                <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-display font-semibold text-[0.7rem] tracking-[0.05em] uppercase text-bark-50">Country</th>
                <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-display font-semibold text-[0.7rem] tracking-[0.05em] uppercase text-bark-50">Homicide Rate</th>
                <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-display font-semibold text-[0.7rem] tracking-[0.05em] uppercase text-bark-50 hidden sm:table-cell">Context</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr class="border-b border-bark-100 bg-white">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-bark-600">Costa Rica</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-bark-800 font-semibold">12.8&ndash;23.0</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-bark-500 hidden sm:table-cell">Often cited as "safe" &mdash; actually much higher</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-bark-100 bg-bark-50">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-bark-600">Uruguay</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-bark-800 font-semibold">11.2</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-bark-500 hidden sm:table-cell">Considered among the safest in South America</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-bark-100 bg-white">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-bark-600">Paraguay</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-bark-800 font-semibold">5.7&ndash;7.0</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-bark-500 hidden sm:table-cell">Comparable to Chile</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-bark-100 bg-bark-50">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-bark-600">Chile</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-bark-800 font-semibold">4.6&ndash;6.7</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-bark-500 hidden sm:table-cell">Regional gold standard</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-bark-100 bg-accent/5">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-bark-800 font-semibold">Bolivia</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-accent font-bold text-lg">4.1&ndash;4.3</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-bark-700 font-medium hidden sm:table-cell">Among the lowest in the Americas</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <p class="text-bark-400 text-xs mt-2">
            Sources:
            <a href="https://data.worldbank.org/indicator/VC.IHR.PSRC.P5?locations=BO" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-accent/70 hover:text-accent underline">World Bank / UNODC</a>,
            <a href="https://insightcrime.org/news/insight-crime-2024-homicide-round-up/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-accent/70 hover:text-accent underline">InSight Crime 2024</a>
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="ml-16 mt-4 bg-bark-100 rounded-xl p-6 border border-bark-200">
          <p class="text-bark-600 text-sm leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-bark-800">Critical finding:</strong> Crime does not spike catastrophically
            during crises. The 2019 political deaths (36 people) were concentrated in two specific
            incidents (Sacaba and Senkata massacres by security forces) &mdash; not indicative of
            general social breakdown. Petty crime against foreigners remains a real concern, but
            Bolivia does not become Colombia when things get hard.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Factor 4: Institutional Resilience -->
      <div class="mb-12 reveal" data-delay="150">
        <div class="flex items-start gap-4 mb-4">
          <div class="flex-shrink-0 w-12 h-12 rounded-xl bg-accent/10 flex items-center justify-center">
            <span class="font-display font-bold text-accent text-lg">4</span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 flex-wrap">
              <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800 text-lg">Institutional Resilience Through Political Drama</h3>
              <span class="tag">Moderate</span>
            </div>
            <p class="text-bark-600 text-[1rem] leading-[1.8] mt-3">
              Bolivia has had a president ousted (2019), a coup attempt that collapsed in three hours (2024),
              a ruling party implosion from 60% to 3% (2025), and through all of it &mdash; democratic elections
              continued, power transferred, and mass civil war never materialized. That is genuinely remarkable.
            </p>
            <p class="text-bark-600 text-[1rem] leading-[1.8] mt-3">
              The counter-argument is real: tanks in the plaza is not a sign of healthy institutions. Bolivia
              has had over 190 coups, revolutions, and coup attempts since independence in 1825. The pattern is crisis, near-collapse, and recovery
              &mdash; not stability. <strong class="text-bark-800">Bolivia bends but does not break.</strong>
              The relevant question: will it descend into Syria-style collapse? Almost certainly no. Will it
              be chaotic and uncomfortable? Almost certainly yes.
            </p>
            <p class="text-bark-400 text-xs mt-3">
              Sources:
              <a href="https://www.as-coa.org/articles/reaction-bolivias-failed-coup-attempt-and-what-it-means" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-accent/70 hover:text-accent underline">2024 coup attempt (AS/COA)</a>,
              <a href="https://www.as-coa.org/articles/explainer-bolivias-2025-elections" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-accent/70 hover:text-accent underline">AS/COA 2025 elections</a>
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Factor 5: Food Sovereignty Potential -->
      <div class="mb-12 reveal" data-delay="150">
        <div class="flex items-start gap-4 mb-4">
          <div class="flex-shrink-0 w-12 h-12 rounded-xl bg-accent/10 flex items-center justify-center">
            <span class="font-display font-bold text-accent text-lg">5</span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 flex-wrap">
              <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800 text-lg">Food Sovereignty Potential</h3>
              <span class="tag">Moderate</span>
            </div>
            <p class="text-bark-600 text-[1rem] leading-[1.8] mt-3">
              "Food sovereignty" is in Bolivia's constitution since 2009. The reality is more complicated:
              Bolivia imports roughly 60% of its wheat, over 30% of fresh food, and aimed to be food
              self-sufficient by 2020 without achieving it. But the majority of domestically produced
              fresh food (estimates range from 80&ndash;90%, varying by source and definition) comes from
              small-scale family farms &mdash; a decentralized food system that is harder to disrupt
              than industrial agriculture.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Factor 6: Community Structures -->
      <div class="mb-4 reveal" data-delay="150">
        <div class="flex items-start gap-4 mb-4">
          <div class="flex-shrink-0 w-12 h-12 rounded-xl bg-accent/10 flex items-center justify-center">
            <span class="font-display font-bold text-accent text-lg">6</span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 flex-wrap">
              <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800 text-lg">Indigenous Community Resilience &amp; Ayllu System</h3>
              <span class="tag">Moderate</span>
            </div>
            <p class="text-bark-600 text-[1rem] leading-[1.8] mt-3">
              The <em>ayllu</em> system &mdash; traditional Andean community structure &mdash; operates on
              reciprocal labor (<em>ayni/minka</em>), ecological complementarity, and collective resource
              management that predate the Spanish conquest by centuries. Communities must help neighbors
              with construction or farming; favors are returned. This creates a social safety net
              independent of money or the state.
            </p>
            <p class="text-bark-600 text-[1rem] leading-[1.8] mt-3">
              <strong class="text-bark-800">Caveat:</strong> The ayllu system primarily operates in highland
              Andean communities (Quechua and Aymara). It is less relevant in the Yungas, which has
              Afro-Bolivian and mixed populations. For a foreigner, this advantage is real only to the
              extent they are integrated into local community structures.
            </p>
            <p class="text-bark-400 text-xs mt-3">
              Sources:
              <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ayllu" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-accent/70 hover:text-accent underline">Ayllu (Wikipedia)</a>,
              <a href="https://www.fao.org/social-protection/news-and-events/news/news-detail/fao-initiates-a-project-in-bolivia-to-strengthen-social-protection-for-indigenous-peoples/en" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-accent/70 hover:text-accent underline">FAO Bolivia</a>
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Divider -->
  <div class="divider"></div>

  <!-- Vulnerability Factors -->
  <section class="bg-bark-100 py-24 px-6 lg:px-10">
    <div class="max-w-[1400px] mx-auto">
      <h2 class="text-title text-bark-800 mb-3 reveal">What Bolivia Gets Wrong</h2>
      <p class="text-bark-500 mb-12 max-w-2xl reveal" data-delay="100">
        Vulnerability factors ranked from most to least serious. Ignoring these would be dishonest.
      </p>

      <!-- Vulnerability cards -->
      <div class="space-y-6">

        <!-- V1: Economic Collapse -->
        <div class="bg-bark-50 rounded-xl p-8 border border-bark-200 border-l-4 border-l-ember reveal" data-delay="150">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4 flex-wrap">
            <span class="text-label text-ember text-[0.6rem]">Critical</span>
            <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800 text-lg">Economic Collapse</h3>
          </div>
          <p class="text-bark-600 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed mb-6">
            This is Bolivia's most severe current vulnerability. If a global crisis hits while Bolivia
            is already in economic freefall, the compound effect could be devastating.
          </p>

          <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4 gap-4 mb-4">
            <div class="stat-block bg-bark-100 rounded-lg">
              <div class="stat-value text-ember">~$100M</div>
              <div class="stat-label">Foreign Reserves</div>
              <div class="stat-context">Down from $14B a decade ago</div>
            </div>
            <div class="stat-block bg-bark-100 rounded-lg">
              <div class="stat-value text-ember">20%+</div>
              <div class="stat-label">Inflation (2025)</div>
              <div class="stat-context">Up from ~2% in 2022</div>
            </div>
            <div class="stat-block bg-bark-100 rounded-lg">
              <div class="stat-value text-ember">CCC-</div>
              <div class="stat-label">Credit Rating</div>
              <div class="stat-context">Near-default territory</div>
            </div>
            <div class="stat-block bg-bark-100 rounded-lg">
              <div class="stat-value text-ember">70&ndash;115%</div>
              <div class="stat-label">Dollar Gap</div>
              <div class="stat-context">Official vs. parallel rate</div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p class="text-bark-400 text-xs">
            Sources:
            <a href="https://www.state.gov/reports/2025-investment-climate-statements/bolivia/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-accent/70 hover:text-accent underline">State Dept</a>,
            <a href="https://internationalbanker.com/finance/dwindling-reserves-put-bolivias-economy-on-edge/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-accent/70 hover:text-accent underline">International Banker</a>,
            <a href="https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2025/05/30/pr-25168-bolivia-imf-concludes-2025-art-iv-consult" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-accent/70 hover:text-accent underline">IMF 2025</a>
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- V2: Healthcare -->
        <div class="bg-bark-50 rounded-xl p-8 border border-bark-200 border-l-4 border-l-ember reveal" data-delay="150">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4 flex-wrap">
            <span class="text-label text-ember text-[0.6rem]">Serious</span>
            <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800 text-lg">Healthcare System Weakness</h3>
          </div>
          <p class="text-bark-600 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed">
            WHO ranking: 126th of 190 (year 2000 assessment, most recent available). Only 1.2 doctors per 1,000 people (global average: 2.6).
            During COVID-19, deaths in Bolivia's poorest departments were
            <strong class="text-bark-800">7x higher than normal</strong>.
            In the Yungas specifically, healthcare options are limited to local clinics &mdash;
            serious medical emergencies require road evacuation to La Paz (3&ndash;4 hours).
            No helicopter services. No local hospitals with advanced care.
          </p>
          <p class="text-bark-400 text-xs mt-3">
            Sources:
            <a href="https://www.thelancet.com/journals/langlo/article/PIIS2214-109X(21)00001-2/fulltext" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-accent/70 hover:text-accent underline">Lancet (COVID Bolivia)</a>,
            <a href="https://www.best-country.com/en/south_america/bolivia/medicine" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-accent/70 hover:text-accent underline">Best-Country Healthcare</a>
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- V3: Infrastructure -->
        <div class="bg-bark-50 rounded-xl p-8 border border-bark-200 reveal" data-delay="150">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4 flex-wrap">
            <span class="text-label text-bark-400 text-[0.6rem]">Serious</span>
            <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800 text-lg">Infrastructure Fragility</h3>
          </div>
          <p class="text-bark-600 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed">
            The Yungas is reachable by one main road from La Paz. If that road is cut by a landslide
            or political blockade, the region is isolated. During the rainy season (November&ndash;March),
            convective storms exceeding 100mm in 24 hours trigger flash floods and landslides that
            frequently isolate communities. Road blockades during political protests regularly cut
            connections between regions &mdash; this happened in 2019, 2024, and 2025.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- V4: Energy -->
        <div class="bg-bark-50 rounded-xl p-8 border border-bark-200 reveal" data-delay="150">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4 flex-wrap">
            <span class="text-label text-bark-400 text-[0.6rem]">Serious</span>
            <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800 text-lg">Energy Sector in Terminal Decline</h3>
          </div>
          <p class="text-bark-600 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed">
            Gas production dropped from ~60 Mm3/day (2014 peak) to 31.9 Mm3/day (2024). Bolivia now
            imports 86% of its diesel and 54% of its gasoline &mdash; spending $56M/week on fuel imports.
            Electricity generation is 67% natural gas. A fuel crisis is an everything crisis.
            During a global energy crisis, Bolivia would be competing for imported fuel with depleted
            reserves and a collapsing currency.
          </p>
          <p class="text-bark-400 text-xs mt-3">
            Sources:
            <a href="https://www.trade.gov/country-commercial-guides/bolivia-energy" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-accent/70 hover:text-accent underline">US Trade.gov</a>,
            <a href="https://fee.org/articles/running-on-empty/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-accent/70 hover:text-accent underline">FEE</a>
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- V5: Climate -->
        <div class="bg-bark-50 rounded-xl p-8 border border-bark-200 reveal" data-delay="150">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4 flex-wrap">
            <span class="text-label text-bark-400 text-[0.6rem]">Moderate</span>
            <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800 text-lg">Climate &amp; Water Risk</h3>
          </div>
          <p class="text-bark-600 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed">
            The Tuni-Condoriri glaciers (water source for La Paz/El Alto) lost 39% of their area
            between 1983&ndash;2006. The Chacaltaya glacier has completely disappeared. In drought
            years, 84% of regional water comes from glacier melt. But &mdash; and this matters &mdash;
            <strong class="text-bark-800">the Yungas is not glacier-dependent</strong>. It receives
            abundant rainfall year-round (up to 1,350mm annually) with groundwater buffers.
            The climate risk is real for La Paz. It is significantly lower for the Yungas.
          </p>
