  <!-- Specific Scenario: Logistics + Digital + War -->
  <section class="bg-bark-100 py-24 px-6 lg:px-10">
    <div class="max-w-[1400px] mx-auto">
      <h2 class="text-title text-bark-800 mb-3 reveal">The Scenario That Changes Everything</h2>
      <p class="text-bark-500 mb-6 max-w-3xl reveal" data-delay="100">
        Most crisis comparisons assume normal conditions with one variable changed.
        The scenario that actually matters is the one where multiple systems fail simultaneously:
        <strong class="text-bark-800">global air and sea logistics collapse, damage to digital
        infrastructure, and active war between historically warmongering nations</strong> (US/China/Russia/NATO conflicts).
      </p>
      <p class="text-bark-500 mb-12 max-w-3xl reveal" data-delay="200">
        Under this compound scenario, the conventional hierarchy of safe-haven countries inverts.
        Islands become traps. NATO adjacency becomes a liability. Undersea cables become single
        points of failure. And the advantages of being landlocked, non-aligned, and already
        adapted to scarcity suddenly look different.
      </p>

      <!-- Scenario country analysis -->
      <div class="space-y-6 mb-12">

        <!-- New Zealand -->
        <div class="bg-bark-50 rounded-xl p-8 border border-bark-200 reveal" data-delay="200">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4">
            <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800 text-lg">New Zealand</h3>
            <span class="tag">GPI #4 &mdash; but under this scenario?</span>
          </div>
          <p class="text-bark-600 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed mb-3">
            Peacetime champion. Under compound stress, the picture shifts.
            New Zealand is a remote island nation that <strong class="text-bark-800">imports 100% of
            its refined fuel</strong> and depends entirely on sea logistics for manufactured goods,
            medicine, and industrial supplies. Its food production is massive but export-oriented
            &mdash; converting to domestic consumption takes time and policy that may not arrive
            during a fast-moving crisis.
          </p>
          <p class="text-bark-600 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed mb-3">
            Digital infrastructure routes through undersea cables &mdash; precisely the chokepoints
            damaged in this scenario. As a Five Eyes member (intelligence alliance with the US, UK,
            Australia, Canada), New Zealand is not neutral: it has intelligence-sharing agreements,
            hosts signals intelligence facilities, and could be targeted or blockaded in a US/China
            Pacific conflict. The distance that protects it in peacetime isolates it during a
            sea-route collapse.
          </p>
          <p class="text-bark-600 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-bark-800">Compound scenario verdict:</strong> Food-secure but
            fuel-dependent, digitally vulnerable via cable infrastructure, and politically aligned
            in ways that create targeting risk. An island fortress with no drawbridge.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Uruguay -->
        <div class="bg-bark-50 rounded-xl p-8 border border-bark-200 reveal" data-delay="200">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4">
            <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800 text-lg">Uruguay</h3>
            <span class="tag">Stable and democratic &mdash; but small and dependent</span>
          </div>
          <p class="text-bark-600 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed mb-3">
            Excellent food producer, strong democratic institutions, no military alliances.
            But Uruguay's economy is structurally dependent on Argentina and Brazil &mdash; its two
            giant neighbors. If those economies destabilize (likely in a global logistics collapse),
            Uruguay's trade routes, supply chains, and currency stability all suffer. Uruguay
            also depends on port access for virtually all non-regional trade.
          </p>
          <p class="text-bark-600 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-bark-800">Compound scenario verdict:</strong> Food-secure, non-aligned,
            but economically small and dependent on neighbors who would be in severe distress.
            Better than most, but its advantages are largely peacetime advantages.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Paraguay -->
        <div class="bg-bark-50 rounded-xl p-8 border border-bark-200 reveal" data-delay="200">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4">
            <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800 text-lg">Paraguay</h3>
            <span class="tag">Landlocked, agricultural, cheap</span>
          </div>
          <p class="text-bark-600 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed mb-3">
            Similar structural advantages to Bolivia: landlocked (already adapted to non-maritime
            trade), agricultural economy, low cost of living, no military alliances. Paraguay is
            a major food producer (soybeans, beef) with genuine food self-sufficiency.
            But Paraguay's agriculture is industrialized and export-oriented &mdash; heavily dependent
            on agrochemical imports and machinery. Its community resilience structures are weaker
            than Bolivia's indigenous networks.
          </p>
          <p class="text-bark-600 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-bark-800">Compound scenario verdict:</strong> Good structural position
            (landlocked, food-producing, non-aligned) but less community resilience and more
            dependent on industrial agriculture inputs that would become scarce.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Bolivia / Yungas -->
        <div class="bg-bark-50 rounded-xl p-8 border border-bark-200 border-l-4 border-l-accent reveal" data-delay="200">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4">
            <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800 text-lg">Bolivia (Yungas specifically)</h3>
            <span class="tag tag-accent">Already adapted to scarcity</span>
          </div>
          <p class="text-bark-600 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed mb-3">
            Under this specific scenario, Bolivia's chronic weaknesses become paradoxical strengths.
            <strong class="text-bark-800">Landlocked = already independent of sea logistics.</strong>
            Bolivia's trade is 14% Brazil, 11% Argentina, 10% Peru &mdash; all accessible by overland
            routes. It never had sea access, so losing it changes nothing. Its container costs are
            already 55.7% higher than Chile's; it has already adapted to that markup.
          </p>
          <p class="text-bark-600 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed mb-3">
            No military alliances. No intelligence-sharing agreements. No strategic bases.
            Constitution prohibits foreign military presence. In a US/China/Russia conflict,
            Bolivia is invisible &mdash; no targeting value, no alliance obligations, no reason
            for any belligerent to notice it exists.
          </p>
          <p class="text-bark-600 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed mb-3">
            The Yungas specifically: year-round food production without industrial inputs
            (small-scale farming, not agrochemical-dependent monoculture). Abundant water
            independent of glaciers. Community structures that have survived 500 years of
            colonial and post-colonial disruption.
          </p>
          <p class="text-bark-600 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-bark-800">The trade-offs remain real:</strong> weak healthcare,
            economic crisis, fragile infrastructure, single-road dependency. Bolivia does not
            become Switzerland under stress. But it does not collapse the way maritime-dependent
            economies do when the ships stop coming.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

