              <td class="px-3 py-3 text-bark-600 text-xs">High (port-dependent for non-regional trade)</td>
              <td class="px-3 py-3 text-accent font-semibold text-xs">Low (landlocked; overland routes to Brazil, Argentina)</td>
              <td class="px-3 py-3 text-accent font-semibold text-xs">None (landlocked; overland to 5 neighbors; never had sea access)</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-8 bg-bark-50 rounded-xl p-8 border border-bark-200 border-l-4 border-l-accent reveal" data-delay="300">
        <p class="text-bark-700 text-[1rem] leading-relaxed mb-3">
          <strong class="text-bark-800">What the table shows:</strong> Under this specific compound
          scenario, Bolivia's position improves relative to its peacetime ranking. New Zealand's
          greatest peacetime asset (island remoteness) becomes its greatest liability when ships stop
          coming. Bolivia's chronic weakness (landlocked, no sea access) becomes irrelevant &mdash;
          because sea access is precisely what has collapsed.
        </p>
        <p class="text-bark-700 text-[1rem] leading-relaxed mb-3">
          Bolivia still loses on healthcare, economic stability, and energy. It wins on military
          target risk (zero), sea logistics dependency (zero), and community cohesion (centuries-deep).
          It ties or leads on communications resilience (overland fiber to five neighbors, no undersea
          cable dependency).
        </p>
        <p class="text-bark-700 text-[1rem] leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-bark-800">The asymmetry is clear:</strong> Bolivia's weaknesses are
          things a community can partially address (build solar, stockpile medicine, grow food).
          New Zealand's weakness under this scenario &mdash; being an island when the sea routes are
          gone &mdash; cannot be addressed at any scale.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Scenario Analysis -->
