  <!-- The Real Pitch -->
  <section class="py-24 px-6 lg:px-10">
    <div class="max-w-[1400px] mx-auto">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
        <h2 class="text-title text-bark-800 mb-6 text-center reveal">The Real Argument</h2>

        <blockquote class="border-l-3 border-accent pl-6 py-4 bg-accent/5 rounded-r-xl my-10 reveal" data-delay="100">
          <p class="text-bark-700 font-display font-medium text-[1.2rem] leading-relaxed italic">
            Staying with the trouble requires learning to be truly present, not as a vanishing pivot
            between awful or edenic pasts and apocalyptic or salvific futures, but as mortal critters
            entwined in myriad unfinished configurations of places, times, matters, meanings.
          </p>
          <p class="text-bark-400 text-sm mt-3 not-italic">&mdash; Donna Haraway, <em>Staying with the Trouble</em></p>
        </blockquote>

        <p class="text-bark-600 text-[1.1rem] leading-[1.85] mb-6 reveal" data-delay="200">
          BARK is not selling Bolivia as the safest place on Earth. The comparison table above makes
          that claim untenable. If safety is your only criterion, buy a house in New Zealand or Iceland
          and be done with it. What BARK argues is different.
        </p>
        <p class="text-bark-600 text-[1.1rem] leading-[1.85] mb-6 reveal" data-delay="300">
          The question is not where to <em>hide</em> but where to <em>build capability</em>.
          Resilience is not a geographic feature you consume. It is a practice you develop.
          A community that grows its own food, generates its own power, manages its own water,
          and governs itself does not need Bolivia to be safe &mdash; it needs Bolivia to be
          <em>affordable enough and left-alone enough</em> to do the work.
        </p>
        <p class="text-bark-600 text-[1.1rem] leading-[1.85] mb-6 reveal" data-delay="400">
          Bolivia offers that. Land costs a fraction of Costa Rica. The legal framework recognizes
          cooperatives as first-class entities. The Yungas provides year-round food production
          and abundant water. The government, perpetually preoccupied with its own crises, largely
          leaves small communities to manage themselves. That is not a bug. For our purposes,
          it is the primary feature.
        </p>

        <div class="pull-quote reveal" data-delay="500">
          Not a bunker. A workshop. Not a safe haven. A place where you can practice
          the skills that make any place safer.
        </div>

        <p class="text-bark-600 text-[1.1rem] leading-[1.85] mb-6 reveal" data-delay="600">
          Anna Tsing writes about life in the ruins of capitalism &mdash; about the organisms and
          communities that flourish not in spite of disturbance but <em>through</em> it.
          Matsutake mushrooms growing in the wreckage of industrial forests. Collaborative survival
          in damaged landscapes. BARK takes this insight seriously: the world is not going to become
          safe. The question is how to live well and build capacity in a world that is already
          precarious.
        </p>
        <p class="text-bark-600 text-[1.1rem] leading-[1.85] reveal" data-delay="700">
          Bolivia, with all its fractures and all its stubborn refusal to collapse entirely,
          is a place where that question can be answered with your hands in the soil.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

